// A shape with no edges: space folded into cells, with a ball and two beams in
// every one of them.
//
// `examples/melt_blob.kir` is the other field here and is one object — a sphere,
// a box and a ring, at three known places. This one is a *pattern*: a single
// `mod` maps every point in the world into one cell, so what the three lines
// below describe once is drawn everywhere at once, and what the marcher meets is
// a structure it can travel through rather than an object it can go round.
//
//     karakuri-cli --set sphere_shell.kir,beam_lattice.kir,glow_march.kir \
//                  --edge glow_march.shape=beam_lattice
//
// **It does not fit under `examples/field_lens.kir`, and that is worth knowing
// before reaching for it.** That marcher takes 34 steps, this field costs 103
// ops per evaluation, and the pair estimates at 4450 ops/fragment against the
// 4096 ceiling — refused where the Set is built, with both figures in the
// message and neither file over on its own. `glow_march` takes 26 steps and
// fits at 3710. This is the trade the specification names: a cheaper field buys
// back steps, and a marcher's step count is chosen against the field it is
// pointed at. `melt_blob` at 82 ops fits under both.
//
// **Domain repetition is `mod` and a subtraction, and nothing else.** Shifting
// by half a cell before the fold and back after it is what centres the cell on
// the origin; without the shift the ball sits at a corner and the lattice is
// off by half in every direction. This language's `mod` is the non-negative one,
// so the fold is correct on the negative side of the origin as well — which
// `%` in WGSL would not be, and is the reason the specification says the
// lowering inserts a wrapper.
//
// **The beams are boxes longer than a cell.** In folded space the coordinate
// never leaves one cell, so a box whose half-extent along z is the whole cell
// width has no ends: it meets the next cell's copy of itself and the two read as
// one continuous rod. Two of them, along z and along x, make the frame.
//
// **`bound` is what keeps it finite.** An infinite lattice fills the frame edge
// to edge and has no silhouette, so it is intersected with a sphere — `max` of
// two distances, which is what `op_intersect` is — and the result is a ball of
// structure sitting where the specification's viewing conditions ask material to
// be. Turning `bound` up past 4 puts the camera inside the lattice, which is a
// second look this one file has in it.
//
// **Cost is per evaluation and the caller pays it as many times as it marches.**
// The four things below that are worth an op — the fold, three SDF calls and two
// CSG operators — come to 103, and every one of them is a step some marcher
// cannot take. Dropping `weld` for a hard union takes it to 95; dropping `pulse`
// as well takes it to 89, which is what it would cost to fit under a 34-step
// marcher. Both were kept: a field that breathes and welds is worth more here
// than eight extra steps of somebody else's march.

proc beam_lattice {
  kind Field

  // The spacing of the lattice. Small is a fine mesh and expensive to march
  // through — a marcher stepping by the distance to the nearest surface takes
  // small steps in a fine structure — and large is a few big pieces. Below
  // about 0.7 the structure is finer than the step count can resolve and the
  // surface starts to break up, which is the honest lower bound.
  param cell   : float [0.5, 4.0] = 2.0

  // Radius of the ball at the centre of each cell. It has to stay under half
  // the cell or the balls merge into a solid and there is no lattice left; that
  // is a real setting rather than a broken one, and it is why the range reaches
  // 1.0 while the default is a third of it.
  param ball   : float [0.05, 1.0] = 0.34

  // Half-thickness of the two rods. At 0 they vanish and the picture is a cloud
  // of loose balls; the top of the range is where the rods are thicker than the
  // balls and the lattice reads as a grid of tubes with lumps on it.
  param beam   : float [0.0, 0.4] = 0.09

  // Radius of the sphere the lattice is cut out of.
  param bound  : float [0.5, 4.0] = 3.0

  // How much the balls swell and shrink, as a fraction of their radius. The
  // whole lattice breathes together, which is what a marcher sees change from
  // frame to frame while the camera holds still.
  param pulse  : float [0.0, 1.0] = 0.25

  // How softly the balls join the rods. At 0 the joint is a crease; at 1 the
  // lattice is a single melted mass and the cell structure is barely readable.
  param weld   : float [0.0, 1.0] = 0.25

  field {
    // The clock is the caller's, handed in at the call site — a field cannot
    // know how the procedure evaluating it spells `t`, so reading it here is
    // reading what that caller passed.
    let breathe = 1.0 + pulse * sin(t * 1.1);

    // The fold. Every point in the world lands in one cell centred on zero.
    let half = cell * 0.5;
    let q    = mod(point + vec3(half), vec3(cell)) - vec3(half);

    let sph  = sd_sphere(q, ball * breathe);
    let rod0 = sd_box(q, vec3(beam, beam, cell));
    let rod1 = sd_box(q, vec3(cell, beam, beam));

    let frame = op_union(rod0, rod1);
    let lat   = op_smooth_union(sph, frame, weld);

    distance = op_intersect(lat, sd_sphere(point, bound));
  }
}
